<div id="2026-11-02-aisystem-merge-trial-state" class="cl-entry">
<h3>2026-11-02 — main merged into aisystem: trial-state conflicts resolved</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>What.</strong> The halted <code>main</code> &rarr; <code>aisystem</code> merge was completed (<code>3cd825463</code>). One real conflict, in runtime trial state <code>data/ai_trials/gpt_56_luna.state.json</code>: the <code>aisystem</code> side was kept (<code>tasks_done 10</code>, stopped at the probation limit, <code>last_checked_at 1793637921</code>) because <code>script/ai_trial.pl</code> only ever appends newer snapshots and <code>main</code> held the older <code>tasks_done 6</code> copy. A second defect arrived <em>tracked</em> on <code>main</code>: <code>data/ai_trials/kimi_k27_code.state.json</code> carried <code>&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; Stashed changes</code> markers inside the JSON from an earlier stash pop; the payloads were identical, so the clean copy was restored. The same merge removed committed conflict markers from <code>Controller/Api.pm</code> and brought in the git dashboard's target-aware redirects (<code>_git_back</code>), the <code>_git_list</code> argv runner, guard hysteresis (<code>clear_streak</code>), <code>trial_flags</code>, and the shared paid-step rule for several probation models.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Why.</strong> Committing the pre-merge state would have shipped two broken files at once: an invalid JSON trial state (the dashboard and commit gate validate that file, so every later commit would have failed) and a trial record that under-reported sessions done. Trial state is runtime data, not source, so the newer snapshot is the only correct resolution and no reviewer pass is needed for it.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Follow-on fix (same session).</strong> The new <code>Comserv/data/ai_trials/</code> ignore rule made the dashboard's Commit fail with <em>&quot;Stage-before-commit failed: The following paths are ignored … use -f&quot;</em>. Cause: <code>git add</code> refuses a path that a <em>later</em> ignore rule matches even when the file is already tracked, and all three trial state files are still tracked. Fix: <code>-f</code> is now an allowed flag for <code>add</code> in <code>Util::Git</code>, and both dashboard staging sites (<code>op=stage</code> and stage-before-commit) pass it. This cannot widen what gets staged — ignored <em>untracked</em> files never appear in <code>git status --porcelain</code>, and the controller validates every path against that output before staging.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Files.</strong> <code>Comserv/data/ai_trials/gpt_56_luna.state.json</code>, <code>Comserv/data/ai_trials/kimi_k27_code.state.json</code>, <code>.gitignore</code>, <code>Comserv/lib/Comserv/Controller/Admin/Git.pm</code>, <code>Comserv/lib/Comserv/Util/Git.pm</code>, <code>Comserv/lib/Comserv/Controller/Api.pm</code>, <code>Comserv/lib/Comserv/Util/AI/HermesOverrunGuard.pm</code>, <code>Comserv/lib/Comserv/Util/AI/ModelChains.pm</code>, <code>Comserv/root/admin/git/index.tt</code>, <code>Comserv/root/static/js/git-dashboard.js</code>, <code>Comserv/t/controller_Admin_Git.t</code>.</p>
</div>
